# supercronic reads this file inside the backup container.
#
# Times are container-local and TZ is pinned to UTC in the image, so "03:15"
# means 03:15 UTC regardless of where the server sits or whether DST is in play.
#
# supercronic passes each script's exit status through, so a failure is visible in
# the container log on top of the ntfy alert the scripts raise themselves.

# Nightly encrypted backup. 03:15 avoids the top of the hour, when every other
# cron job on a home server tends to run.
15 3 * * * /usr/local/lib/backup/backup.sh

# Daily staleness check. This catches "the schedule quietly stopped firing", which
# is the most common way a backup system fails without anyone noticing. It needs no
# key, so it is always safe to run unattended.
0 12 * * * /usr/local/lib/backup/restore.sh --check-staleness

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The weekly restore drill is NOT scheduled by default.
#
# It needs the age private key, and this design deliberately keeps that key off
# the server so that a compromised server cannot decrypt its own backups. A cron
# entry here would therefore fail every week with "AGE_IDENTITY_FILE is not set".
#
# Run it deliberately, from the operator's side, by mounting the key for one run:
#
#   docker compose -f compose.backup.yml run --rm \
#     -e AGE_IDENTITY_FILE=/run/secrets/age_identity \
#     -v ~/pf-backup-identity.txt:/run/secrets/age_identity:ro \
#     verify --mode=drill
#
# To automate it instead, read "Automating the drill" in backup/README.md and
# accept the tradeoff it describes. Then uncomment this line:
#
# 30 4 * * 0 /usr/local/lib/backup/restore.sh --mode=drill
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
